  "mask_assignment_rule": "Sort all 21 position_id values lexicographically (ascending, plain string sort). Assign mask = 0x10 + index, where index is the 0-based rank in that sorted order. This gives the contiguous range 0x10..0x24 with no gaps and no reuse. The assignment is fixed once here and is never reassigned after images are generated -- regenerating an image with a different mask would invalidate the recorded sha256. Recomputable from this file alone: sorted(p['position_id'] for p in positions) then mask[i] = 0x10 + i reproduces every entry's mask exactly.",
  "stamp_width_rule": {
    "w27c512": 16,
    "w29c020": 32,
    "reason": "w27c512 is 65536 B (16 address bits) -- a 16-bit stamp covers the whole address space, so attribution is complete and Phase 145's exact recipe applies unchanged. w29c020 is 262144 B (18 address bits) -- a 16-bit stamp would repeat every 65536 bytes and leave an A16/A17 aliasing fault invisible to address attribution (the exact fault class that root-caused Phase 97's pin-31/A18-aliasing defect), so the 32-bit rotating-word stamp is required on this part to make such a fault attributable rather than merely detectable."
  },
  "artifact_volume_policy": {
    "decision": "Committed: the six built .hex images and SHA256SUMS.txt (D-04, a separate artifact set from this file), IMAGE-PLAN.json itself, every flash_readback.bin (32768 B each, ~0.7 MB total across 20 bring-up-style flash proofs, not reproducible from any record), every provenance.json, EVIDENCE.jsonl, EVIDENCE.md and the text logs. Not committed: the 21 generated WRITTEN images this file describes (each is exactly reproducible from gen_addr_image.py plus this file's recorded size_bytes/mask/stamp_width, and the recorded sha256 is a far smaller and equally strong artifact than committing 3.2+ MB of bytes) and the chip read-back run_*.bin files produced by dev consistency-check (~6.5 MB total, SHAs recorded in EVIDENCE.jsonl, files kept locally under bench/cells/<cell_slug>/reads/).",
    "committed_on_failure_exception": "EXCEPTION: for any position whose judged verdict is not a clean match -- a SHA mismatch, an N=3 disagreement, or a verdict disagreement against the app's unjudged 0/1/2 -- the run_*.bin files AND the written.bin for that position ARE committed (via `git add -f`, since bench/.gitignore excludes them by default), because those are the exact bytes Phase 165's root-cause analysis needs. This exception is the reason bench/.gitignore's excluded paths need a stated escape hatch rather than a blanket, unconditional exclusion.",
    "resulting_non_claim": "Non-claim, stated explicitly: a clean-match chip read-back is re-checkable by SHA against the record, not by re-reading its actual bytes back out of this repository -- the bytes themselves exist only locally under bench/cells/<cell_slug>/reads/ unless that position's verdict was not a clean match, in which case the bytes are committed and both forms of re-check are available."
  },
